      }\r\n        \r\n        /// <summary>\r\n        ///   Looks up a localized string similar to Er is een fout opgetreden bij het initialiseren van de gebruikersinstellingen. Probeer de applicatie (later) opnieuw op te starten of neem contact op met de helpdesk.\r\n        /// </summary>\r\n        public static string UserPreferences_FailedToInitialize {\r\n            get {\r\n                return ResourceManager.GetString(\"UserPreferences_FailedToInitialize\", resourceCulture);\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n        \r\n        /// <summary>\r\n        ///   Looks up a localized string similar to Er is een fout opgetreden bij het aanpassen van de instellingen, probeer het opnieuw of neem contact op met de helpdesk..\r\n        /// </summary>\r\n        public static string UserPreferences_FailedToUpdate {\r\n            get {\r\n                return ResourceManager.GetString(\"UserPreferences_FailedToUpdate\", resourceCulture);\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n```\r\n", "raw_llm_output": "{\n  \"explanation\": \"This class centralizes a very large number of unrelated responsibilities and message domains in one place (attachments, budgets, product cards, NMD queries, error pages, user preferences, etc.). Even though it is generated code, it still behaves like a God Class from a design perspective: many parts of the system depend on one oversized type, so any change to localization keys, naming, grouping, or culture handling affects a broad surface area. The global static Culture property also introduces shared mutable state, which can create hidden coupling between callers and make behavior context-dependent. As the resource set grows, the class becomes harder to navigate, reason about, and safely evolve.\",\n  \"quality_impact\": \"Quality impact:\\n- Maintainability: A single oversized resource wrapper mixes many functional areas, making the codebase harder to understand and organize. Developers must search through one large class for unrelated messages, which increases cognitive load.\\n- Analysability: Finding which messages belong to which feature, detecting duplicates, and understanding impact of changes becomes more difficult because all keys are exposed through one monolithic API.\\n- Modifiability: Adding, renaming, or removing resource entries has wide ripple effects because many modules depend on the same class. Refactoring toward clearer feature boundaries is harder once everything is coupled to one type.\\n- Testability: The static access pattern and shared mutable Culture state make tests less isolated and can cause order-dependent behavior when tests run in parallel or change culture settings.\\n- Reliability: Global mutable localization state can lead to inconsistent message resolution across threads or requests if culture is changed unexpectedly, especially in multi-user or asynchronous scenarios.\",\n  \"remediation\": \"Split resources by bounded context or feature area, and avoid exposing one monolithic static class to the whole application. Keep generated .resx wrappers small and domain-focused, for example AttachmentErrors, BudgetErrors, ProductCardErrors, and SharedErrors. Prefer injecting a localization abstraction instead of reading static properties directly, and avoid process-wide mutable culture state; pass CultureInfo explicitly when needed or rely on request/thread culture managed by the framework.\\n\\nConcrete steps:\\n1. Create separate .resx files per feature/module.\\n2. Regenerate strongly typed resource classes for each file.\\n3. Replace direct references to ErrorsResource with the appropriate feature-specific class or an injected localizer.\\n4. Remove or stop using the static Culture setter; use CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture or pass culture explicitly.\\n5. If many callers only need lookup by key, introduce a small interface to decouple consumers from generated classes.\\n\\nExample:\\n```csharp\\npublic interface IErrorTextProvider\\n{\\n    string AttachmentEmpty(CultureInfo? culture = null);\\n}\\n\\npublic sealed class AttachmentErrorTextProvider : IErrorTextProvider\\n{\\n    public string AttachmentEmpty(CultureInfo? culture = null) =>\\n        AttachmentErrors.ResourceManager.GetString(\\n            nameof(AttachmentErrors.Attachment_Empty),\\n            culture ?? CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture) ?? string.Empty;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nOr, if keeping generated classes:\\n```csharp\\n// AttachmentErrors.resx -> generated AttachmentErrors class\\nvar message = AttachmentErrors.Attachment_Empty;\\n\\n// BudgetErrors.resx -> generated BudgetErrors class\\nvar exportError = BudgetErrors.BudgetFile_Export;\\n```\\n\\nThis reduces coupling, improves discoverability, and limits the impact of future localization changes to the relevant module.\"\n}", "input_tokens": 7267, "output_tokens": 721, "duration_ms": 12446.2868, "parsed_explanation": "This class centralizes a very large number of unrelated responsibilities and message domains in one place (attachments, budgets, product cards, NMD queries, error pages, user preferences, etc.). Even though it is generated code, it still behaves like a God Class from a design perspective: many parts of the system depend on one oversized type, so any change to localization keys, naming, grouping, or culture handling affects a broad surface area. The global static Culture property also introduces shared mutable state, which can create hidden coupling between callers and make behavior context-dependent. As the resource set grows, the class becomes harder to navigate, reason about, and safely evolve.", "parsed_quality_impact": "Quality impact:\n- Maintainability: A single oversized resource wrapper mixes many functional areas, making the codebase harder to understand and organize. Developers must search through one large class for unrelated messages, which increases cognitive load.\n- Analysability: Finding which messages belong to which feature, detecting duplicates, and understanding impact of changes becomes more difficult because all keys are exposed through one monolithic API.\n- Modifiability: Adding, renaming, or removing resource entries has wide ripple effects because many modules depend on the same class. Refactoring toward clearer feature boundaries is harder once everything is coupled to one type.\n- Testability: The static access pattern and shared mutable Culture state make tests less isolated and can cause order-dependent behavior when tests run in parallel or change culture settings.\n- Reliability: Global mutable localization state can lead to inconsistent message resolution across threads or requests if culture is changed unexpectedly, especially in multi-user or asynchronous scenarios.", "parsed_suggested_fix": "Split resources by bounded context or feature area, and avoid exposing one monolithic static class to the whole application. Keep generated .resx wrappers small and domain-focused, for example AttachmentErrors, BudgetErrors, ProductCardErrors, and SharedErrors. Prefer injecting a localization abstraction instead of reading static properties directly, and avoid process-wide mutable culture state; pass CultureInfo explicitly when needed or rely on request/thread culture managed by the framework.\n\nConcrete steps:\n1. Create separate .resx files per feature/module.\n2. Regenerate strongly typed resource classes for each file.\n3. Replace direct references to ErrorsResource with the appropriate feature-specific class or an injected localizer.\n4. Remove or stop using the static Culture setter; use CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture or pass culture explicitly.\n5. If many callers only need lookup by key, introduce a small interface to decouple consumers from generated classes.\n\nExample:\n```csharp\npublic interface IErrorTextProvider\n{\n    string AttachmentEmpty(CultureInfo? culture = null);\n}\n\npublic sealed class AttachmentErrorTextProvider : IErrorTextProvider\n{\n    public string AttachmentEmpty(CultureInfo? culture = null) =>\n        AttachmentErrors.ResourceManager.GetString(\n            nameof(AttachmentErrors.Attachment_Empty),\n            culture ?? CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture) ?? string.Empty;\n}\n```\n\nOr, if keeping generated classes:\n```csharp\n// AttachmentErrors.resx -> generated AttachmentErrors class\nvar message = AttachmentErrors.Attachment_Empty;\n\n// BudgetErrors.resx -> generated BudgetErrors class\nvar exportError = BudgetErrors.BudgetFile_Export;\n```\n\nThis reduces coupling, improves discoverability, and limits the impact of future localization changes to the relevant module.", "parsed_risks": null, "expected_quality_attributes": ["Maintainability", "Modularity", "Analysability", "Modifiability"], "llm_quality_attributes_mentioned": ["Analysability", "Maintainability", "Modifiability", "Modularity", "Reliability", "Testability"], "quality_attribute_alignment": 1, "quality_attribute_notes": null, "parse_status": "ok", "error_status": null, "error_message": null, "selected_from_run_number": 1, "presentation_index": 5, "blinding_lot": "pilot-a5961646", "selection_seed": 1}
